 "notes": "40 stations, numbered 1-40, all on one level with no sub-headings. The section is IRISH COAST: AUTUMN., but the list itself prints no coast heading, so coast is null throughout. Supply \"Irish Coast\" from the section heading if you want one. Fragments have leading and trailing spaces trimmed; spacing inside a line is kept as printed. The list names no keepers, describes no light characters and prints no marks or italics.\n\nColumns: \"other\" holds the five data columns as printed: birds striking lantern, daily entries, species in entries, lantern height above sea level, and miles from shore. Only Fastnet's height has \"ft.\" after it. \"--\" is printed for an empty cell. Barrels Rock's height is printed as \"?\".\n\nRows that need care:\n- Tuskar (7) runs over two lines, 3273-3274. The birds-striking figure is \"large numbers\", split across the two lines; \"other\" joins the pieces.\n- Blackrock (27) has \"Numbers\" in the birds-striking column instead of a count.\n- Samphire Island (36) and Tearaght (37) have \"Do.\", meaning \"No reply.\" like Loop Head above them.\n- Calf Rock (40) has \"Destroyed in storm, Nov. 1881.\" instead of data.\n- \"do.\" in the place field is a ditto for the county in the row above (e.g. Galley Head = Co. Cork). It is kept as printed, not expanded. Wicklow Head (9) has \"do\" with no full stop.\n- Old Head, Kinsale: \"Kinsale\" is taken as part of the name (the following date table names it \"Old Head, Kinsale\"), and \"do.\" (Co. Cork) as the place.\n- Types: only the light-ships carry one, \"Lt.-ship\": Coningbeg (5), Arklow S. (8) and Kish Bank (10). For Arklow the name is \"Arklow S.\" (South light-ship) and the type is \"Lt.-ship\". All the others have no type, so it is null.\n- Spellings kept as printed: \"Howth Baily\" (usually Bailey), \"Innishtrahull\" (Inishtrahull), \"Arran Island N./S.\" (Aran), \"Skelligs\", \"Valentia\", \"Oyster Island, N.\". Directional suffixes (N., S., E., W.) are kept in the names because they mark separate stations, such as the paired Eagle Island and Slyne Head lights.",
